Application code shouldn't think about tiers; one façade abstracts them.

```mermaid
classDiagram
    class CacheManager~K,V~ {
        +get(key, loader) V
        +put(key, value)
        +invalidate(key)
    }
    class CacheLayer~K,V~ {
        <<interface>>
        +get(key) Optional~V~
        +put(key, value, ttl)
        +invalidate(key)
    }
    class CaffeineLayer
    class RedisLayer
    class StampedeGuard {
        +singleflight(key, loader) V
    }
    class InvalidationBus {
        +publish(key)
        +subscribe(handler)
    }
    class TtlPolicy {
        +ttlFor(key) Duration
    }

    CacheManager --> CacheLayer
    CacheManager --> StampedeGuard
    CacheManager --> InvalidationBus
    CacheManager --> TtlPolicy
    CacheLayer <|.. CaffeineLayer
    CacheLayer <|.. RedisLayer
```

**Key code paths:**

```java
public class LayeredCacheManager<K, V> {
    public V get(K key, Supplier<V> loader) {
        Optional<V> l1 = caffeine.get(key);
        if (l1.isPresent()) return l1.get();

        Optional<V> l2 = redis.get(key);
        if (l2.isPresent()) {
            caffeine.put(key, l2.get(), ttl.ttlFor(key));
            return l2.get();
        }

        return guard.singleflight(key, () -> {
            V loaded = loader.get();
            redis.put(key, loaded, ttl.ttlFor(key));
            caffeine.put(key, loaded, ttl.ttlFor(key));
            return loaded;
        });
    }

    public void invalidate(K key) {
        caffeine.invalidate(key);
        redis.invalidate(key);
        bus.publish(key);          // peers evict their L1
    }

    @PostConstruct void subscribe() {
        bus.subscribe(key -> caffeine.invalidate(key));
    }
}
```

Why this shape:

- **`CacheLayer` interface** — swap implementations (in-memory for tests, Caffeine/Redis for prod).
- **`StampedeGuard.singleflight`** — only one caller per key fetches from the source; others wait.
- **`InvalidationBus`** — keeps L1 caches across instances coherent.
- **`TtlPolicy`** centralizes per-key TTL decisions (jitter, per-namespace overrides).

Modern CDNs (Cloudflare Workers, Fastly Compute@Edge, AWS Lambda@Edge) run code at the POP.

**Pattern:** cache the base HTML aggressively; personalize with edge JavaScript that injects user-specific bits (greeting, cart count) using cookies or auth tokens — without round-tripping to origin.

```js
// Cloudflare Worker pseudo
addEventListener('fetch', e => e.respondWith(handle(e.request)));
async function handle(req) {
  const cached = await caches.default.match(req);
  if (!cached) return fetch(req);                                  // origin
  const userId = req.headers.get('cookie')?.match(/uid=(\w+)/)?.[1];
  if (!userId) return cached;
  const html = await cached.text();
  return new Response(html.replace('{{user}}', userId),
    {headers: cached.headers});
}
```

Gives you the cache hit rate of public content with per-user personalization.

- **HashMap** maps key → list node, giving O(1) lookup.
- **Doubly linked list** orders entries by recency; head = MRU, tail = LRU. O(1) move and O(1) eviction.
- On `get`: lookup node → unlink → move to head → return value.
- On `put`: if exists, update value + move to head; if not, create node + add to head; if size > capacity, remove tail node and erase from map.

Sentinel head and tail nodes simplify edge cases (no null checks for empty list / single element).

```java
class Node<K, V> {
    K key; V value;
    Node<K, V> prev, next;
}
```

Doubly linked (not singly) because we need O(1) removal of an arbitrary node — the node found via the map.

```java
public class LruCache<K, V> {
    private final int capacity;
    private final Map<K, Node<K, V>> map;
    private final Node<K, V> head, tail;
    private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();

    public LruCache(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.map = new HashMap<>(capacity * 2);
        this.head = new Node<>(); this.tail = new Node<>();
        head.next = tail; tail.prev = head;
    }

    public V get(K key) {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            Node<K, V> n = map.get(key);
            if (n == null) return null;
            moveToHead(n);
            return n.value;
        } finally { lock.unlock(); }
    }

    public void put(K key, V value) {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            Node<K, V> n = map.get(key);
            if (n != null) {
                n.value = value;
                moveToHead(n);
                return;
            }
            n = new Node<>(); n.key = key; n.value = value;
            map.put(key, n);
            addToHead(n);
            if (map.size() > capacity) {
                Node<K, V> lru = tail.prev;
                removeNode(lru);
                map.remove(lru.key);
            }
        } finally { lock.unlock(); }
    }

    private void moveToHead(Node<K, V> n) { removeNode(n); addToHead(n); }
    private void addToHead(Node<K, V> n) {
        n.prev = head; n.next = head.next;
        head.next.prev = n; head.next = n;
    }
    private void removeNode(Node<K, V> n) {
        n.prev.next = n.next; n.next.prev = n.prev;
    }
}
```

Correct, but the single lock serialises every access — even `get`. That's fine for ~10K ops/sec but melts at 1M ops/sec.

**Lazy expiration:** check expiry on access.

```java
public V get(K key) {
    Entry<V> e = map.get(key);
    if (e == null) return null;
    if (e.expiresAt < now()) {
        map.remove(key, e);                  // CAS-style remove
        return null;
    }
    return e.value;
}
```

- Pro: zero overhead for keys that are never read.
- Con: keys never accessed again sit in memory forever until something purges them.

**Active expiration:** background process removes expired entries.

- Pro: bounded memory; expired entries actually disappear.
- Con: needs a data structure that tells us "what's expiring next" cheaply.

**Both together is the right answer.** Lazy ensures correctness (a never-active sweeper can't return stale data); active ensures memory bounds.

**Approach 1: Periodic scan of every key.**

```java
scheduler.scheduleAtFixedRate(() -> {
    long now = System.currentTimeMillis();
    for (var e : map.entrySet()) {
        if (e.getValue().expiresAt < now) map.remove(e.getKey());
    }
}, 1, 1, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
```

- O(N) per tick. With 10M keys at 1Hz tick, that's 10M comparisons/sec wasted on keys that aren't expiring.

**Approach 2: One `ScheduledExecutorService` task per key.**

```java
scheduler.schedule(() -> map.remove(key), ttlMs, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
```

- Each scheduled task takes ~200 bytes; 10M keys = 2 GB of scheduler overhead alone.
- Cancelling a task on `setTtl` or `delete` is O(log N) at best.

**Approach 3: `DelayQueue` of expiry events.**

- A single PriorityQueue of `(key, expiresAt)` ordered by expiry.
- Background thread drains expired entries.
- O(log N) insertion (when `put` is called).
- Acceptable up to ~1M pending entries; degrades for very large N.

**Approach 4: Hashed timing wheel (Netty's pattern).**

- Array of buckets representing time slots.
- Insert: O(1) into the right bucket.
- Tick: process the current bucket; entries in that bucket are expired.
- Hierarchical wheels handle large TTL ranges efficiently.

For most use cases, the right balance is **lazy on read + a single background thread draining a `DelayQueue`**.

```java
public class TtlKvStore<K, V> {
    private static class Entry<V> implements Delayed {
        final V value;
        final long expiresAtNs;
        final K key;
        // ... compareTo + getDelay using expiresAtNs
    }

    private final ConcurrentHashMap<K, Entry<V>> map = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private final DelayQueue<Entry<V>> expiryQ = new DelayQueue<>();
    private final Thread sweeper;

    public TtlKvStore() {
        sweeper = new Thread(this::sweepLoop, "ttl-sweeper");
        sweeper.setDaemon(true);
        sweeper.start();
    }

    public void put(K key, V value, long ttlMs) {
        long expiresAtNs = System.nanoTime() + TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS.toNanos(ttlMs);
        Entry<V> e = new Entry<>(key, value, expiresAtNs);
        Entry<V> prev = map.put(key, e);
        expiryQ.offer(e);
        // prev's queue entry will be skipped during drain via map cross-check
    }

    public V get(K key) {
        Entry<V> e = map.get(key);
        if (e == null) return null;
        if (System.nanoTime() >= e.expiresAtNs) {
            map.remove(key, e);
            return null;
        }
        return e.value;
    }

    private void sweepLoop() {
        while (!Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted()) {
            try {
                Entry<V> e = expiryQ.take();          // blocks until next expiry
                Entry<V> current = map.get(e.key);
                if (current == e) {                    // not replaced/removed since
                    map.remove(e.key, e);
                }
            } catch (InterruptedException ie) { return; }
        }
    }
}
```

**Key correctness points:**

- The sweeper checks `current == e` (reference equality) before removing — if `put` replaced the entry, the old DelayQueue entry is stale and skipped.
- Lazy `get` handles the brief window between expiry and sweeper processing.
- Sweeper runs as a daemon thread; doesn't block JVM shutdown.

**When this is enough:** ~1M pending entries with second-to-minute TTLs. CPU overhead minimal.

**When it isn't:** 100M+ entries with millisecond-precision TTLs — the DelayQueue's O(log N) insertion becomes the bottleneck.
